    "q-183753": {
      "dom_id": "step-q183753",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "Use mathematical induction to prove that ${n}^{2}>2n-2$ for $n\\in N.$\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,${{field-1}}.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{field-2}}={{field-3}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is\n{{field-4}}\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is {{field-5}} for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ {{field-6}}, we have\n\n$$\n{{field-7}}>{{field-8}}.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)^{2} & ={{field-9}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}+2k+1 & {{field-10}}{{field-11}} \\\\  & ={{field-12}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & >{{field-13}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${n}^{2}>2n-2$ is true for all\n{{field-14}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}>2n-2\\,\\text{for}\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case. Since we are required to prove the statement for $n\\ge 1,$ the base case is $P(1).$ So, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2}=1,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)-2=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}>2k-2.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n(k+1)^{2}>2(k+1)-2=2k.\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than to the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)^{2} & ={k}^{2}+2k+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}+2k+1 & ={{({k}^{2})}_{⏟}}_{>2k-2}+2k+1 \\\\  & >2k-2+2k+1 \\\\  & =2k+{{2k-1}_{⏟}}_{>0} \\\\  & >2k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ is greater than the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${n}^{2}>2n-2$ for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nPROOF\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove that our statement is true for the base case $n=\\,$$1$.\n\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is\ngreater than\nthe right-hand side.\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=$ $k\\ge 1$, we have\n\n$$\n{k}^{2}>2k-2.\n$$\n\n**Inductive Step**: To prove $P(k+1),$ we write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)^{2} & ={k}^{2}+2k+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{k}^{2}+2k+1 & >2k-2+2k+1 \\\\  & =2k+2k-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & >2k,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${n}^{2}>2n-2$ is true for all\n$n\\ge 1$.",
